      "id": "ta2-16",
      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>(1) Every year the world produces hundreds of millions of tonnes of plastic, and a large share of it is used only once before being thrown away. Items such as bottles, food wrappers and shopping bags may serve us for a few minutes, yet they can stay in the environment for hundreds of years. Recycling is often presented as the answer to this problem. However, the reality is more complicated than most people imagine.</p><p>(2) To begin with, not all plastic can be recycled in the same way. Plastics are made from different materials, and each type must be sorted before it is melted down. A clear water bottle, for example, can be turned into polyester fabric or new containers, but a dirty food tray or a thin plastic film is much harder to process. When different types are mixed together, the quality of the new product falls, so factories often reject the whole batch. As a result, only a small part of the plastic that people put in their recycling bins is actually made into something new.</p><p>(3) Daily habits also play a role. Many people believe that they can simply drop any plastic item into the bin and let the system do the rest. In fact, a single greasy container can spoil a whole load of clean material. Some cities have therefore launched campaigns asking residents to rinse containers and to check the local rules before they recycle. One small town reduced the amount of rejected waste by almost a third within a year after it sent simple leaflets to every household. The result suggests that clear information can make a real difference.</p><p>(4) Even so, experts agree that recycling alone will not solve the problem. A more effective approach begins with using less plastic in the first place. Some supermarkets now let customers refill their own bottles, and several countries have introduced a small fee for plastic bags, which has cut their use sharply. Companies are also being encouraged to design packaging made from a single material, so that it can be recycled easily. Only when these steps are combined with careful sorting at home will the amount of plastic waste begin to fall, they argue.</p>",
